      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Argatroban, a selective thrombin inhibitor, is expected to decrease acute coronary re-occlusion and restenosis via direct suppression of thrombin generation after percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) in patients with acute myocardial infarction. This study evaluated the effect of argatroban compared with heparin as an adjunctive anticoagulation therapy after PCI in patients with acute myocardial infarction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fifty-four consecutive patients with acute myocardial infarction underwent PCI within 6 hr from the onset and were randomly allocated to receive argatroban (argatroban group: n = 27) or heparin (heparin group: n = 27) after PCI. Each drug was administered intravenously for 72 hr. Coronary angiography was repeated at 1 and 6 months after the onset of acute myocardial infarction. In-hospital cardiac events, bleeding complications, and long-term outcome were surveyed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Baseline clinical and angiographic variables were similar in the two groups. Acute coronary re-occlusion and major hemorrhagic complications did not occur in either group. Minor bleeding complications, such as hematoma of the puncture sites, occurred significantly less frequently in the argatroban group than in the heparin group (4% vs 30%, p < 0.05). After 6 months, there were no significant differences in restenosis rate and target vessel recanalization rate between the two groups. Subgroup analyses revealed no significant differences in restenosis rate between patients treated with only balloon angioplasty and stent implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Argatroban provides similar prevention of acute thrombotic events and restenosis compared with heparin. However, argatroban might reduce bleeding complications in patients with acute myocardial infarction after PCI."
        }
      ]},
